  "id": "Compound::DB00595 - Gene::10864",
  "verbalization": "Oxytetracycline, a member of the tetracycline class of antibiotics, is implicated in the transport of organic anions through the cell membrane, as evidenced by its binding to Slc22a6 and Slc22a11, which are both involved in organic anion transport. This mechanism is supported by the presence of Slc22a7, which is also part of the organic anion transport pathway, suggesting a shared functional role in transporting anions across cellular membranes. The relationship between Oxytetracycline and Slc22a7 is further reinforced by the fact that Oxytetracycline binds to Slc22a7 through a common pathway, indicating a direct interaction that may influence the transport process. The evidence from the three paths collectively suggests that Oxytetracycline exerts a modulatory effect on Slc22a7 through the organic anion transport system, potentially affecting the uptake or efflux of anions in cellular processes. The integration of these findings supports a mechanistic link between Oxytetracycline and Slc22a7, highlighting the role of the organic anion transport system in mediating the drug's effects.",
